{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='Intervals.icu workout_doc heart rate target bpm value end Garmin zones' numResults=5\"","aggregated_output":"Title: Exact HR corridors on Garmin from Intervals.icu API workouts — start/end, not value/end — Worklore\nURL: https://worklore.dev/s/2026-05-12-exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts\nPublished: 2026-05-12T01:15:47.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n# Exact HR corridors on Garmin from Intervals.icu API workouts — start/end, not value/end\n\nMy AI agent generates structured triathlon workouts and pushes them to Intervals.icu via `POST /events`; Intervals FIT-exports them to my Garmin watch. The symptom: every HR corridor on the watch was silently wrong — clamped to the watch's own (stale, auto-detected) LTHR zones instead of the range we planned. I misdiagnosed it as a watch-side LTHR mismatch for weeks.\n\nWhat we tried, each round-tripped push → API readback → Garmin sync → watch face:\n\n1. `{units: \"bpm\", value, end}` — API accepted; watch showed 214–249 bpm (impossible). FIT export misread `bpm` as percent-of-something.\n2. `{start_bpm, end_bpm}` — watch showed a constant 101, Garmin's \"no valid HR target\" fallback. Schema silently dropped.\n3. Surveyed the Intervals UI target dropdown, concluded \"absolute bpm is impossible\". Wrong — that dropdown picks the units label, not the value shape.\n\nThe breakthrough: build a workout in the Intervals UI with its \"Instant HR\" toggle, then read it back via the API. The corridor's lower bound is `start`, not `value`. That one key name switches the FIT export mode: `{value, end}` routes as a Lap-HR / zone-mapped target (the watch clamps it to its local zones), while `{start, end}` becomes an Instant-HR corridor — the watch displays the raw bpm Intervals computed. Verified end-to-end: Intervals LTHR 172, watch LTHR 168, a 75–82% target rendered 129–141 bpm on the watch — matching Intervals, drift-immune. The fix was a one-word rename after days of probing.\n\n...\n\nBonus trap from the same session: steps were invisible in the Intervals web UI, because the UI reads a separate top-level `description` in Intervals' text grammar — where `m` means minutes (`200m` = a 200-minute step!), distance must be `mtr`/`km`, a bare `Z2` resolves to power zones even for swims, and a digit at the start of a label gets eaten as a duration. If the parser chokes, Intervals wipes both representations.\n\n...\n\nPr ... .\n\n...\n\nwill need from ... your ... ; permission to\n\n...\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Create an event via `POST /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events` with `workout_doc.steps`, where every HR step target is `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\":, \"end\":}` — never `value`. Percentages, not absolute bpm: Intervals converts at FIT-export time from its own stored threshold.\n2. Set the event's top-level `description` in Intervals' native text grammar so steps render in the web UI, e.g. `- Warm-up easy 10m 75-82% LTHR`. Rules: durations `m`/`s`, distance `mtr`/`km`, blank lines around `Nx` repeat blocks, no digits at the start of labels, no bare `Z ` outside cycling.\n3. Read the event back with `GET /events/{id}`: non-empty `workout_doc.steps` means the parser accepted the description; `steps: []` means it choked.\n4. Sync the watch through Garmin Connect and open the scheduled workout.\n\nVerify: the watch shows a bpm corridor equal to your percentages × the LTHR stored in Intervals — not the watch's own LTHR (easiest to prove when the two differ). The Intervals web UI shows the full step chart instead of just a name and total duration.\n\n---\n\nTitle: API access to Intervals.icu\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609?page=20\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThanks, sadly I can’t use raw values (watts\\bpm). I push these values to TrainingPeaks, and it accepts only percentage of ftp\\lthr\\etc.\n\n...\n\nHmm. For power the athlete’s FTP is included in the workout_doc field:\n\n```auto\n    \"workout_doc\": {\n      \"duration\": 3600,\n      \"distance\": 0.0,\n      \"ftp\": 290,\n      \"target\": \"POWER\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        {\n          \"duration\": 3600,\n          \"power\": {\n            \"value\": 2.0,\n            \"units\": \"power_zone\"\n          },\n          \"_power\": {\n            \"start\": 162.0,\n            \"end\": 217.0\n          }\n        }\n      ],\n      \"zoneTimes\": [..],\n      \"options\": {},\n      \"locales\": []\n    },\n\n```\n\nSo you can use that to translate the _power step into % of FTP. If that works I can add lthr and threshold_pace as well.\n\n...\n\n```\n\n...\n\n{\n\n...\n\n\"duration\": 6 ... 0,\n  \"power ... {\n    \"value\": 40.0\n\n...\n\n\"units\": \"%ftp\"\n  },\n\n...\n\n\": {\n    \"value\": ... .0,\n    \"start\": 9 ... .0,\n    \"end\": 99.0\n  }\n},\n\n...\n\nYes, Intervals.icu translates fixed power (and other) targets into a range. For power this is only done for outdoor workouts. The default range for power and pace is 2.5%. For HR it is 1.5%. If the athlete has changed Garmin stuff in /settings then these are the defaults:\n\nYou can also supply powerRange, hrRange and paceRange query parameters to the call (e.g. `powerRange=5.0`) for 5%. Use 0.0 to disable range. I am going to update the docs.\n\nDevices beep and so on if you aren’t in the range and this is impossible outdoors and for HR and pace if there isn’t some spread.\n\nI have added these fields to workout_doc and will deploy Thurs AM (GMT+2):\n\n```\npublic Integer lthr;\npublic Float threshold_pace;\npublic String pace_units;\n\n```\n\n---\n\nTitle: Power and HR targets for Garmin workouts\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/power-and-hr-targets-for-garmin-workouts/2926\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYou can now specify power and HR targets for workout steps (default is lap power and lap HR):\n\nThis sorts out the issue with HR steps not working on Garmin watches (well at least some of them). You need to choose “Instant HR” (hr=1s in the text):\n\n- warmup 30s 50% LTHR hr=1s\n- Wibble 20m 78% LTHR hr=1s\n- cooldown 30s 40% LTHR hr=1s\n\n...\n\nDo you plan to add this functionality also to the pace based workouts? By default it is lap average but I’d much rather it would be 3 second average.\n\n...\n\nUnfortunately the target in the Garmin API is just “PACE”. There aren’t other options like there are for power.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Workout builder - Page 24 - Guide - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/workout-builder/1163?page=24\nPublished: 2020-06-16T11:49:37.000Z\nAuthor: Howie\nHighlights:\nSure I will send you some files. For both Garmin and Suunto you supply a JSON doc.\n\n...\n\nNo the format is quite different. But all the worked out power/bpm ranges in the JSON download will help.\n\n...\n\n@david the HR ramps need some loving. but the Power one is fixed.\n\nIn my post here Building workout using zone number instead of percentage range - #24 by app4g,\n\nScreenshot 2022-04-17 at 10.35.43 PM 1216×1270 188 KB\n\nIt’s from this Test workout \n\nScreenshot 2022-04-17 at 10.28.44 PM 1432×778 50 KB\n\n```auto\n[\n  {\n    \"duration\": 300,\n    \"ramp\": true,\n    \"hr\": {\n      \"start\": 100,\n      \"end\": 50,\n      \"units\": \"%hr\"\n    },\n    \"_hr\": {\n      \"value\": 174,\n      \"start\": 116,\n      \"end\": 232\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"duration\": 300,\n    \"ramp\": true,\n    \"hr\": {\n      \"start\": 50,\n      \"end\": 100,\n      \"units\": \"%hr\"\n    },\n    \"_hr\": {\n      \"value\": 174,\n      \"start\": 116,\n      \"end\": 232\n    }\n  }\n]\n\n```\n\nGood news now is that the workout shown on Intervals will be similar as workout shown in BreakAway. HR Ramps will be plotted as a Ramp, but internally it’s a range. HR Range will be plotted as a flat line (ave of range) but internally will still be a range)\n\nHR Target Based Training feature, using workouts sync’ed from intervals.icu is finally nearly there.\n\n...\n\n18 April ... 02 ... 1 ... 75 \n\nFixed. Ramps are now kept for HR and pace. Will deploy Tues AM (GMT+2).\n\n...\n\nTX BTW, for confirmation, are the zones start and end supposed to be at the same BPM? eg: Z1 = 100-120 Z2 = 121-140 Z3 = 141-160\n\nso ramp Z1 -Z2 then Z2-Z3 should be 100-120 → 121-140 → 141-160 or will it be middle of the HR Zone? Just want to ... and it’s not me ... -interpreting the way it should be.\n\n...\n\nnew targets (distance ... time etc ... is quite ... lot of risky work unfortunately. Your best bet now is to just start with steps that gets you enough kJ of work and then put in the real stuff. 3h on the lower side of Z2 is 2000 kJ for me:\n\n...\n\ndavid 19 April 2022 04:23 483 \n\nThe zone ranges go from the start of the zone to the end of the zone. So if you to “Z2-Z3” its lowest bpm (or watts) in Z2 to highest in Z3. Just using “Z2” is lowest in Z2 to highest in Z2.\n\nZ1 is a special case with no lower bound so 80% of the upper bound is used.\n\n...\n\nYou can’t do a down ramp down using a single zone specifier.\n\nIt is behaving as expected for the up ramps. The first part of the range (Z2-Z3) uses the lower end of Z2 but the end of the range uses the upper end of Z3. So the next range starting on Z3 will not line up with the end of the first.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Workout builder - Page 56 - Guide - Intervals.icu Forum\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/workout-builder/1163?page=56\nPublished: 2020-06-16T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nFor your “special” case yes.\n\n...\n\nBut in general, you can’t write `70%` or `1m` as a textual description. It’s always interpreted as intensity or duration, regardless on which position. duration will sum up (you can write `1m 2m` and that will sum up to 3 minutes), intensity will be overwritten by the latter one. If you really want to “repeat” the intensity and duration as description it’s best to keep an space between number and unit, and keep the order ` `, like:\n`- 70 % MMP 1 min 1m 70% MMP` (Description: `70 % MMP 1 min`) or\n`- 70 % MMP 1 m 1m 70% MMP` (Description: `70 % MMP 1 m`)\n\n...\n\nYes on the first question.\nThe graph will populate with Power/HR/Pace targets.\n\n> @Workout Builder Syntax Quick Guide:\n> \n> Intervals.icu Workout Builder Syntax Quick Guide Use this as a short cheat sheet when writing workout lines. 1) Basic line format Most steps follow this pattern: - [duration OR distance] [target] [optional cadence] Examples: - 5m30s 60% 90rpm - 1km 70% HR - 500mtr 5:00/km Pace 2) Duration and distance Time Hours: 1h Minutes: 10m, 5m Seconds: 30s, 90s Combined: 1h2m30s, 5m30s Short form: 5', 30\", 1'30\" Distance Metric: 500mtr, 2km, 10km Imperial: 1mi, 4.5mi Important m means minutes (…\n\n...\n\nG\n\n...\n\nPost date ... 31 ... 026 1 ... :\n\n...\n\nHow can I set absolute BPM values?\n\nFor example, 10 minutes between 127 BPM and 135 BPM. These don’t exactly match the established zones.\n\n...\n\nAuthor: @Gerald\nPost date: 31 March 2026 18:27 UTC\n\nCurrently, that isn’t possible.\nYou can only use these options for HR:\n\n...\n\n026\n\n...\n\n> @david:\n> \n> ```auto\n> Warmup\n> - 20m 60% 90-100rpm\n> \n> Main set 6x\n> - 4m 100% 40-50 rpm, power is less important than getting the torque\n> - 5m recovery at 40%\n> \n> Cooldown\n> - 20m 60% 90-100rpm\n> \n> \n> ```\n\n...\n\nRecovery ... they ... shown ... the\n\n...\n\nThe load is too different (17 vs 52). Compliance just compares load. Drag and drop the workout to the activity to match then manually.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
