{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"mcporter call exa.web_search_exa query='site:forum.intervals.icu workout_doc hr \\\"start\\\" \\\"end\\\" \\\"%lthr\\\" Garmin' numResults=5\"","aggregated_output":"Title: API access to Intervals.icu\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609?page=22\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nI am assuming you are using this endpoint to get planned workouts: GET /api/v1/athlete/{id}/events{format}\n\nYou can specify resolve=true to get actual watts, bpm and m/s values for power, hr and pace steps. These take into account the sport and the athlete’s settings for that sport at that time. This option was only added earlier this year.\n\n...\n\nIf you specify resolve=true then the FTP, LTHR or threshold pace used to convert the workout steps into watts, bpm or m/s are included in the workout_doc. These are the athlete’s current settings for the sport.\n\n```auto\n\"workout_doc\": {\n    \"description\": \"A ride done with power\",\n    \"duration\": 3120,\n    \"distance\": 0.0,\n    \"ftp\": 290,\n    \"lthr\": 171,\n    \"target\": \"POWER\",\n\n```\n\nA run done with pace:\n\n```auto\n\"workout_doc\": {\n    \"duration\": 3600,\n    \"distance\": 8310.0,\n    \"lthr\": 171,\n    \"threshold_pace\": 2.7777777,\n    \"pace_units\": \"MINS_KM\",\n    \"target\": \"PACE\",\n    \"steps\": [\n\n```\n\n...\n\nHi, first a HUGE thanks for providing\n\n---\n\nTitle: Custom zones work started\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/custom-zones-work-started/112132?page=4\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nWorkout prescription using custom zones will be deployed soon. This does have implications for people using workouts from the API. The “value”, “start” and “end” fields on “power”, “pace” and “hr” objects can now be a string (instead of a number) when a custom zone is selected.\n\nIn this example “cvm” indicates the custom zone with id “VM”.\n\n```auto\n{\n  \"duration\": 1620,\n  \"distance\": 0.0,\n  \"ftp\": 285,\n  \"lthr\": 171,\n  \"sportSettings\": { \"id\": 28723, ... },\n  \"target\": \"POWER\",\n  \"steps\": [\n    {\n      \"duration\": 300,\n      \"power\": { \"value\": 3, \"units\": \"power_zone\" },\n      \"_power\": { \"start\": 214.0, \"end\": 256.0 }\n    },\n    {\n      \"duration\": 120,\n      \"power\": { \"value\": \"cvm\", \"units\": \"power_zone\" },\n      \"_power\": { \"start\": 330.0, \"end\": 360.0 }\n    }\n  ],\n  \"zoneTimes\": [...],\n  \"options\": {},\n  \"locales\": []\n}\n\n```\n\n...\n\nYes the “value”, “start” and “end” fields can be both a string and a number if the units is “*_zone”.\n\nThe “_power”, “_hr” and “_pace” objects always have only numbers (the actual watts, bpm or mps with all custom zones and so on resolved).\n\n---\n\nTitle: Suunto guides: heart-rate step targets show no target and no gauge on the watch, pace targets do\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/suunto-guides-heart-rate-step-targets-show-no-target-and-no-gauge-on-the-watch-pace-targets-do/131169\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nOn a workout synced to a Suunto 9 Peak, a step whose target is pace shows the step\nname, `Avg`, `Target`, the value and the gauge. A step whose target is heart rate\nshows the step name and the ordinary running fields only – no target, no range, no\ngauge, nothing that says what to hold.\n\nI could not tell from the watch whether this was my syntax, so I built one workout with\neleven steps, one target form each, and walked through all of them:\n\n| step | written as | on the watch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 zone | `90s Z3 HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 2 pctmax one | `90s 85% HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 3 pctmax rng | `90s 82-87% HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 4 thr one | `90s 90% LTHR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 5 thr rng | `90s 88-94% LTHR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 6 zone span | `90s Z2-Z3 HR` | no target, no gauge |\n| 7 zone p | `90s Z2 Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 8 pct p one | `90s 40% Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 9 pct p rng | `90s 60-70% Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 10 abs p | `90s 7:00 pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n| 11 dist | `400mtr Z2 Pace` | Avg + Target + gauge |\n\nAll six heart-rate forms behave identically. All five pace forms work.\n\n...\n\n## Why I think the data is not the problem\n\nIntervals resolves every one of those HR steps to absolute bpm – they are shown that way\nin the workout view, and they are in the workout export\n(`/api/v1/athlete/{id}/events/{id}/download.json`) as a `_hr` object:\n\n```auto\n1 zone \"_hr\": {\"start\": 153.0, \"end\": 163.0}\n2 pctmax one \"_hr\": {\"value\": 159.0, \"start\": 156.0, \"end\": 162.0}\n3 pctmax rng \"_hr\": {\"value\": 158.5, \"start\": 154.0, \"end\": 163.0}\n4 thr one \"_hr\": {\"value\": 154.0, \"start\": 151.0, \"end\": 157.0}\n5 thr rng \"_hr\": {\"value\": 156.0, \"start\": 151.0, \"end\": 161.0}\n6 zone span \"_hr\": {\"start\": 146.0, \"end\": 163.0}\n\n```\n\nAnd the SuuntoPlus Guide format has a field for exactly this shape. From Suunto’s own\nGuide Cloud API document, the sample guide’s warm-up step:\n\n```json\n\"fields\": [\n  { \"type\": \"targetHeartRate\", \"value\": 111, \"min\": 104, \"max\": 148 },\n  { \"type\": \"heartRate\" },\n  ...\n]\n\n```\n\n`targetHeartRate` takes absolute bpm as a value with optional min/max, and Suunto’s own\nexample uses it on a heart-rate step.\n\n...\n\nYou could try to set it in Intervals as ‘Auto’.\nI don’t know if a mix will then work in Suunto, but there was recently some work done to get this supported on Garmin.\n\nIt’s strange that we don’t see those selectors on your screenshot. If you select HR, the Intervals graph should show the workout coloured in HR zones. If Pace is selected, it would be in Pace zone colors. Auto\n\n---\n\nTitle: Downloading planned workouts from the API\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/downloading-planned-workouts-from-the-api/93737\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nYou can download planned workouts from an athlete’s calendar in zwo (Zwift), fit, mrc and erg format (CALENDAR:READ scope required):\n\n```auto\n$ curl 'https://intervals.icu/api/v1/athlete/0/events?category=WORKOUT&ext=zwo&resolve=true' \\\n    -H 'Authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9'\n\n```\n\nThe optional category parameter specifies what sort of events to return (planned workouts in this case). The ext parameter the format (leave out if you don’t need the file). The resolve=true parameter converts power (and heart rate etc.) from units like % of FTP into watts. The default date range is a week from today (use oldest and newest parameters in ISO-8601 format to change).\n\n...\n\nExample abridged response:\n\n```auto\n[\n  {\n    \"id\": 46696127,\n    \"start_date_local\": \"2024-11-21T07:00:00\",\n    \"type\": \"Ride\",\n    \"category\": \"WORKOUT\",\n    \"name\": \"Big Gear Grinds\",\n    \"description\": \"...\",\n    ...\n    \"workout_doc\": {\n      \"description\": \"A great session for building cycling specific strength both in and out of the saddle.\",\n      \"target\": \"POWER\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        {\n          \"text\": \"Welcome to the ...\",\n          \"duration\": 180,\n          \"warmup\": true,\n          \"ramp\": true,\n          \"power\": { \"start\": 35.0, \"end\": 55.0, \"units\": \"%ftp\" },\n          \"cadence\": { \"value\": 85.0, \"units\": \"rpm\" },\n          \"_power\": { \"value\": 126.0, \"start\": 98.0, \"end\": 154.0 }\n        },\n      ],\n      ...\n    },\n    ...\n    \"workout_filename\": \"Big_Gear_Grinds.zwo\",\n    \"workout_file_base64\": \"PD94bWwgdmVy...\"\n  }\n]\n\n```\n\nThe workout_file_base64 field contains the file in the specified format (zwo in this case) encoded as base 64 text.\n\nThe workout_doc field contains the workout in native Intervals.icu format. If you don’t already have a parser for one of the other formats then this might be easier to work with.\n\nThis is how the workout_doc field is mapped in Intervals.icu:\n\n```java\npublic class WorkoutDoc {\n\n    public String description;\n    public Map<String, String> description_locale;\n    public Integer duration;\n    public Float distance;\n    public Integer ftp;\n    public Integer lthr;\n    public Float threshold_pace; // meters/sec\n    public String pace_units; // SECS_100M, SECS_100Y, MINS_KM, MINS_MILE, SECS_500M\n    public SportSettings sportSettings;\n    public String category;\n    public WorkoutTarget target;\n    public List<Step> steps;\n    public List<Object> zoneTimes; // sometimes array of ints otherwise array of objects\n    public Map<String, String> options;\n    public List<String> locales;\n\n    public enum Option {\n        category, pool_length, power\n    }\n\n    public static class Step {\n        public String text;\n        public Map<String, String> text_locale;\n        public Integer duration;\n        public Float distance;\n        public Boolean until_lap_press;\n        public Integer reps;\n        public Boolean warmup;\n        public Boolean cooldown;\n        public String intensity; // \"active\", \"rest\", \"warmup\", \"cooldown\", \"recovery\", \"interval\", \"other\"\n        public List<Step> steps;\n        public Boolean ramp;\n        public Boolean freeride;\n        public Boolean maxeffort;\n        public Value power;\n        public Value hr;\n        public Value pace;\n        public Value cadence;\n        public Boolean hidepower;\n\n        // these are filled in with actual watts, bpm etc. when resolve=true parameter is supplied to the endpoint\n        public Value _power;\n        public Value _hr;\n        public Value _pace;\n        public Float _distance;\n    }\n\n    public static class Value {\n        public Double value;\n        public Double start;\n        public Double end;\n        public String units;\n        public String target;\n        public Integer mmp_duration;\n    }\n}\n\n/** How should workouts be performed? Target power, HR or pace? */\npublic enum WorkoutTarget {\n    AUTO(\"A\"), POWER(\"W\"), HR(\"H\"), PACE(\"P\");\n}\n\n```\n\n---\n\nTitle: API access to Intervals.icu\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609?page=20\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nHmm. For power the athlete’s FTP is included in the workout_doc field:\n\n```auto\n    \"workout_doc\": {\n      \"duration\": 3600,\n      \"distance\": 0.0,\n      \"ftp\": 290,\n      \"target\": \"POWER\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        {\n          \"duration\": 3600,\n          \"power\": {\n            \"value\": 2.0,\n            \"units\": \"power_zone\"\n          },\n          \"_power\": {\n            \"start\": 162.0,\n            \"end\": 217.0\n          }\n        }\n      ],\n      \"zoneTimes\": [..],\n      \"options\": {},\n      \"locales\": []\n    },\n\n```\n\nSo you can use that to translate the _power step into % of FTP. If that works I can add lthr and threshold_pace as well.\n\n...\n\nYes, Intervals.icu translates fixed power (and other) targets into a range. For power this is only done for outdoor workouts. The default range for power and pace is 2.5%. For HR it is 1.5%. If the athlete has changed Garmin stuff in /settings then these are the defaults:\n\nYou can also supply powerRange, hrRange and paceRange query parameters to the call (e.g. `powerRange=5.0`) for 5%. Use 0.0 to disable range. I am going to update the docs.\n\nDevices beep and so on if you aren’t in the range and this is impossible outdoors and for HR and pace if there isn’t some spread.\n\nI have added these fields to workout_doc and will deploy Thurs AM (GMT+2):\n\n```\npublic Integer lthr;\npublic Float threshold_pace;\npublic String pace_units;\n\n```\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
